  "scoring_convention": "Absence of disclosure scores 2, never 1. No subdimension here scores 1. Every movement rests on a specific documented count from the federal defect docket, not on missing disclosure. Correction Willingness (AB2) is deliberately held at 2 rather than 1 because GM is not obliged to expand a recall while an Engineering Analysis is open — the absence of an expansion is NOT scored as documented refusal.",
  "measures_absence_not_misconduct": false,
  "key_evidence": [
    "NHTSA opened Engineering Analysis EA26005 on 20 August 2026 covering 997,743 GM vehicles (model years 2021-2026) fitted with the 6.2L L87 V8.",
    "499 owners reported engine failure in vehicles that had ALREADY received the recall repair under Recall 25V-274 — 473 after an oil-viscosity change and 26 after a complete engine replacement. The remedy did not work.",
    "A further 191 failures were reported in engines built after GM's 31 May 2024 recall cutoff, for 690 new failure reports in total. The failure mode recurred in post-cutoff production.",
    "Independent analyses attribute the root cause to microscopic ridges on the L87 crankshaft causing severe oil-pressure fluctuation — i.e. most owners received an oil change for a suspected metal defect.",
    "NO INJURY EVIDENCE WAS LOCATED in any source. The documented harm is loss of the vehicle and a repair that did not hold. Several subdimensions are scored at 2 rather than 1 for this reason, and Boundaries is held at 2.80.",
    "ATTRIBUTION: NHTSA opening the investigation is the regulator's act and is NOT scored as GM conduct. What is scored is GM's engine, GM's chosen remedy, and GM's response to 690 subsequent reports."
